Open a worktree in a browser editor

In the tile every "Open in" entry is disabled ("Local only"), so there is no way
to open a worktree in an editor at all. An entry could only be a local shell
command, which would spawn a process on this machine against a path that lives
on another one, so the runtime disables all of them whenever a runtime
environment is set - which it always is for a web client.

This adds an optional `url` to `OpenInApplication`, with `{path}` substituted by
the worktree's absolute path. A browser editor served by the host that owns the
path has neither problem, so URL entries skip the guards and open through
`shell.openUrl` on desktop and web alike. Nothing deployment-specific enters
Orca: the slug and domain live in the template string the operator writes.
Templates are restricted to http and https, entries are seedable from the
runtime and editable in Settings, and both "Open in" menus draw them from one
entry model so a URL row cannot render enabled in one and dead in the other.

To test, add a Web URL entry in Settings > Open In Apps and open a worktree with
it; then seed the same entry from the runtime and confirm a fresh browser gets
it. Covered by `open-in-url-template.test.ts`, `open-in-applications.test.ts`,
`OpenInMenuSetting.test.ts`, `OpenInMenuRow.test.tsx`,
`WorktreeOpenInMenu.test.tsx`, `external-editor-open-capability.test.ts`,
`client-ui.test.ts` and `runtime-seeded-settings.test.ts`.

A row that carries a URL always gets the full editor, even when its id or label
matches a preset: collapsing it to the command field would hide the URL that
actually decides what the row opens, and leave it unclearable. `OpenInMenuRow`
covers both sides of that gate.

Note for a reader weighing this for upstream: rendering a URL row issues a
favicon request to `www.google.com`. That is upstream's existing behaviour for
presets; this patch extends it to URL rows, which are exactly the rows a
self-hosted deployment adds.

